      "description": "UConn Careers | UConn Careers | University of Connecticut Our websites may use cookies to personalize and enhance your experience.  By continuing without changing your cookie settings, you agree to this collection.  For more information, please see our University Websites Privacy Notice . OK More Information Skip to Navigation Skip to UConn Search Skip to Content Our websites may use cookies to personalize and enhance your experience. By continuing without changing your cookie settings, you agree to this collection. For more information, please see our University Websites Privacy Notice . UConn Cookie Information What are Cookies? Authentication Cookies Analytics Cookies Opt Out Web cookies (also called HTTP cookies , browser cookies , or simply cookies ) are small pieces of data that websites store on your device (computer, phone, etc.) through your web browser. They are used to remember information about you and your interactions with the site. Purpose of Cookies: Session Management : Keeping you logged in Remembering items in a shopping cart Saving language or theme preferences Personalization : Tailoring content or ads based on your previous activity Tracking & Analytics : Monitoring browsing behavior for analytics or marketing purposes Types of Cookies: Session Cookies : Temporary; deleted when you close your browser Used for things like keeping you logged in during a single session Persistent Cookies : Stored on your device until they expire or are manually deleted Used for remembering login credentials, settings, etc. First-Party Cookies : Set by the website you're visiting directly Third-Party Cookies : Set by other domains (usually advertisers) embedded in the website Commonly used for tracking across multiple sites Authentication cookies are a special type of web cookie used to identify and verify a user after they log in to a website or web application. What They Do: Once you log in to a site, the server creates an authentication cookie and sends it to your browser. This cookie: Proves to the website that you're logged in Prevents you from having to log in again on every page you visit Can persist across sessions if you select \"Remember me\" What's Inside an Authentication Cookie? Typically, it contains: A unique session ID (not your actual password) Optional metadata (e.g., expiration time, security flags) Analytics cookies are cookies used to collect data about how visitors interact with a website . Their primary purpose is to help website owners understand and improve user experience by analyzing things like: How users navigate the site Which pages are most/least visited How long users stay on each page What device, browser, or location the user is from What They Track: Some examples of data analytics cookies may collect: Page views and time spent on pages Click paths (how users move from page to page) Bounce rate (users who leave without interacting) User demographics (location, language, device) Referring websites (how users arrived at the site) Here’s how you can disable cookies in common browsers: 1. Google Chrome Open Chrome and click the three vertical dots in the top-right corner. Go to Settings > Privacy and security > Cookies and other site data . Choose your preferred option: Block all cookies (not recommended, can break most websites). Block third-party cookies (can block ads and tracking cookies). 2. Mozilla Firefox Open Firefox and click the three horizontal lines in the top-right corner. Go to Settings > Privacy & Security . Under the Enhanced Tracking Protection section, choose Strict to block most cookies or Custom to manually choose which cookies to block. 3. Safari Open Safari and click Safari in the top-left corner of the screen. Go to Preferences > Privacy . Check Block all cookies to stop all cookies, or select options to block third-party cookies. 4. Microsoft Edge Open Edge and click the three horizontal dots in the top-right corner. Go to Settings > Privacy, search, and services > Cookies and site permissions . Select your cookie settings from there, including blocking all cookies or blocking third-party cookies. 5. On Mobile (iOS/Android) For Safari on iOS: Go to Settings > Safari > Privacy & Security > Block All Cookies . For Chrome on Android: Open the app, tap the three dots, go to Settings > Privacy and security > Cookies . Be Aware: Disabling cookies can make your online experience more difficult. Some websites may not load properly, or you may be logged out frequently. Also, certain features may not work as expected. UConn University of Connecticut Search University of Connecticut Search UConn A to Z Index UConn A to Z Index Site A-Z UConn A-Z UConn Careers Search this Site Search in https://wp.newemployees.hr.uconn.edu/> Search UConn Careers Apply for Jobs Applicant Login Career Portal Help Subscribe to Job Alerts Refine Your Search Filter Results Work type Full-time 1 Locations UConn Storrs 1 Categories Information Technology 1 Quantum CT Evaluation Project Manager Apply now Search #: 499814 Work type: Full-time Location: UConn Storrs Categories: Information Technology JOB SUMMARY The Evaluation Project Manager will be responsible for assisting with day-to-day operations of all evaluation-related activities. They will also help manage evaluation meeting logistics and facilitation, implementation, data management and analyses, supervision of undergraduate and graduate students, and evaluation dissemination and use activities. Job responsibilities cluster into five categories: project management and coordination, evaluation design, evaluation implementation and data analysis, reporting, and quality assurance. The Evaluation Project Manager will report to Dr. Bianca Montrosse-Moorhead, incoming Department Head and Professor in the Department of Educational Psychology, and QuantumCT Engine External Evaluation Lead. The NSF Quantum Technologies Engine in Connecticut (QuantumCT Engine), led by the University of Connecticut, aims to advance American quantum innovation and secure the domestic quantum supply chain by accelerating the commercialization of quantum technologies for national defense, biotechnology, and financial services. Through innovation, applied research leading to new technologies, support for inventors and entrepreneurs and workforce development, the QuantumCT Engine will advance quantum sensing, secured communications, computing, and materials through shared test bed, deep-tech incubator, and translation pathways. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Assist and coordinate the day-to-day operational management of project evaluation activities. Coordinate and support all evaluation-related project logistics, including meetings, workshops, surveys, interviews, focus groups, and stakeholder engagement activities. Support the design, implementation, and management of data collection efforts, including surveys, interviews, assessments, and other evaluation instruments. Maintain evaluation databases and records, ensuring data accuracy, completeness, confidentiality, and compliance with applicable policies and protocols. Compile, organize, analyze, and summarize evaluation data for reports, presentations, and project deliverables. Assist in preparing and submitting quarterly and annual evaluation reports to funding agencies, university leadership, and project stakeholders. Coordinate travel arrangements, event logistics, and reimbursement processing for the evaluation team, consultants, and project participants. Schedule meetings, prepare agendas, maintain meeting records, and document action items related to evaluation activities. Support communication and coordination among the external evaluation team and project partners, researchers, industry partners, and other parties. Assist with grant-related documentation, compliance requirements, and record retention activities. Perform related evaluation duties as assigned. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor's degree in evaluation, public policy, education, social sciences, or a related field. Three or more years of related experience managing or coordinating field-based evaluation projects, including tasks associated with evaluation ramp-up, implementation, analysis, and reporting. Demonstrated project management skills, including experience planning and executing evaluation projects, prioritizing tasks, and meeting deadlines in a dynamic, team-oriented environment. Experience working on an evaluation team, including experience designing, collecting, organizing, and maintaining data and records. Experience managing study regulatory processes, including IRB submissions and amendments, and ensuring adherence to human subjects’ guidelines. Demonstrated organizational skills, with strong attention to detail. Demonstrated written and oral communication skills; experience creating clear and readable documents for diverse audiences, including evaluation reports and presentations. Experience leading and organizing others, including creating and implementing evaluation systems. Demonstrated experience collaborating with stakeholders and constituents from a variety of communities and sectors. Experience working independently under minimal supervision. Demonstrated interpersonal and proactivity skills, including organizational, analytical, and problem-solving skills. Demonstrated experience with Microsoft Office applications, including Excel, Word, PowerPoint, and Teams. Demonstrated experience using survey software (e.g., Qualtrics), quantitative analysis software (e.g., R), and qualitative analysis software (e.g., Dedoose). PREFERRED QUALIFICATION Master's degree is strongly preferred. Project management certification. Experience with Smartsheet, Asana, or equivalent project management tools. Experience managing complex evaluation projects, including addressing challenges that may arise during any phase of the evaluation lifecycle. Demonstrated expertise in systems-informed evaluation, social network methods and analysis, and/or developmental evaluation. Experience with a variety of written dissemination modes for external sponsors, funding agencies, and other interested parties (e.g., evaluation reports of varying lengths, data dashboards, infographics, policy briefs, plain language summaries). Demonstrated experience supporting federally funded programs, grants, or large-scale research initiatives, including financial reporting. Demonstrated experience working with the National Science Foundation, higher education, research, innovation, entrepreneurship, workforce development, or economic development programs. Demonstrated experience with costing projects and managing budgets, forecasts, and invoicing processes. APPOINTMENT TERMS This is a full-time, end-dated position with the possibility of renewal subject to performance and funding. The University offers a competitive salary, and outstanding benefits , including employee and dependent tuition waivers at UConn, and a highly desirable work environment. Other rights, terms, and conditions of employment are contained in the collective bargaining agreement between the University of Connecticut and the University of Connecticut Professional Employees Association (UCPEA). This position is based at the Storrs campus and the successful candidate will be required to work on the campus and travel to other campuses as needed. The salary range for this position is $60,416 to $78,541. TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF EMPLOYMENT Employment of successful candidates will be contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. The successful must be willing and able to travel to in-person meetings locally, statewide, and out-of-state, when needed. TO APPLY Please apply online at Faculty and Staff Positions , Search 499814 and submit the following application materials: (1) A resume or abbreviated CV, 4 pages max, (2) a 2-page cover letter detailing (a) why applicants are interested in this role, and (b) their qualifications relative to those included in the job ad, and contact information for three (3) professional references . Please apply by 11:55 p.m. eastern time on September 28, 2026, for full consideration. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression . The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. Advertised: Sep 01 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Sep 28 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Ok, we will send you jobs like this. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Department Search # Location Closes Quantum CT Evaluation Project Manager Tech Comm and Industry Rltns (Vice President for Research) 499814 UConn Storrs Sep 28 2026 The NSF Quantum Technologies Engine in Connecticut (QuantumCT Engine), led by the University of Connecticut, aims to advance American quantum innovation and secure the domestic quantum supply chain by accelerating the commercialization of quantum technologies for national defense, biotechnology, and financial services. Through innovation, applied research leading to new technologies, support for inventors and entrepreneurs and workforce development, the QuantumCT Engine will advance quantum sensing, secured communications, computing, and materials through shared test bed, deep-tech incubator, and translation pathways.\n\nThe Evaluation Project Manager will be responsible for assisting with day-to-day operations of all evaluation-related activities. They will also help manage evaluation meeting logistics and facilitation, implementation, data management and analyses, supervision of undergraduate and graduate students, and evaluation dissemination and use activities. Job responsibilities cluster into five categories: project management and coordination, evaluation design, evaluation implementation and data analysis, reporting, and quality assurance. The Evaluation Project Manager will report to Dr. Bianca Montrosse-Moorhead, incoming Department Head and Professor in the Department of Educational Psychology, and QuantumCT Engine External Evaluation Lead. Current opportunities Position Department Search # Location Closes Quantum CT Evaluation Project Manager Tech Comm and Industry Rltns (Vice President for Research) 499814 UConn Storrs Sep 28 2026 The NSF Quantum Technologies Engine in Connecticut (QuantumCT Engine), led by the University of Connecticut, aims to advance American quantum innovation and secure the domestic quantum supply chain by accelerating the commercialization of quantum technologies for national defense, biotechnology, and financial services. Through innovation, applied research leading to new technologies, support for inventors and entrepreneurs and workforce development, the QuantumCT Engine will advance quantum sensing, secured communications, computing, and materials through shared test bed, deep-tech incubator, and translation pathways.\n\nThe Evaluation Project Manager will be responsible for assisting with day-to-day operations of all evaluation-related activities. They will also help manage evaluation meeting logistics and facilitation, implementation, data management and analyses, supervision of undergraduate and graduate students, and evaluation dissemination and use activities. Job responsibilities cluster into five categories: project management and coordination, evaluation design, evaluation implementation and data analysis, reporting, and quality assurance. The Evaluation Project Manager will report to Dr. Bianca Montrosse-Moorhead, incoming Department Head and Professor in the Department of Educational Psychology, and QuantumCT Engine External Evaluation Lead. Powered by PageUp © University of Connecticut Disclaimers, Privacy & Copyright Accessibility Webmaster Login"
