    "https://jobs.hr.uconn.edu/cw/en-us/job/499805/director-of-engineering-student-engagement": {
      "title": "Director of Engineering Student Engagement",
      "description": "UConn Careers | UConn Careers | University of Connecticut Our websites may use cookies to personalize and enhance your experience.  By continuing without changing your cookie settings, you agree to this collection.  For more information, please see our University Websites Privacy Notice . OK More Information Skip to Navigation Skip to UConn Search Skip to Content Our websites may use cookies to personalize and enhance your experience. By continuing without changing your cookie settings, you agree to this collection. For more information, please see our University Websites Privacy Notice . UConn Cookie Information What are Cookies? Authentication Cookies Analytics Cookies Opt Out Web cookies (also called HTTP cookies , browser cookies , or simply cookies ) are small pieces of data that websites store on your device (computer, phone, etc.) through your web browser. They are used to remember information about you and your interactions with the site. Purpose of Cookies: Session Management : Keeping you logged in Remembering items in a shopping cart Saving language or theme preferences Personalization : Tailoring content or ads based on your previous activity Tracking & Analytics : Monitoring browsing behavior for analytics or marketing purposes Types of Cookies: Session Cookies : Temporary; deleted when you close your browser Used for things like keeping you logged in during a single session Persistent Cookies : Stored on your device until they expire or are manually deleted Used for remembering login credentials, settings, etc. First-Party Cookies : Set by the website you're visiting directly Third-Party Cookies : Set by other domains (usually advertisers) embedded in the website Commonly used for tracking across multiple sites Authentication cookies are a special type of web cookie used to identify and verify a user after they log in to a website or web application. What They Do: Once you log in to a site, the server creates an authentication cookie and sends it to your browser. This cookie: Proves to the website that you're logged in Prevents you from having to log in again on every page you visit Can persist across sessions if you select \"Remember me\" What's Inside an Authentication Cookie? Typically, it contains: A unique session ID (not your actual password) Optional metadata (e.g., expiration time, security flags) Analytics cookies are cookies used to collect data about how visitors interact with a website . Their primary purpose is to help website owners understand and improve user experience by analyzing things like: How users navigate the site Which pages are most/least visited How long users stay on each page What device, browser, or location the user is from What They Track: Some examples of data analytics cookies may collect: Page views and time spent on pages Click paths (how users move from page to page) Bounce rate (users who leave without interacting) User demographics (location, language, device) Referring websites (how users arrived at the site) Here’s how you can disable cookies in common browsers: 1. Google Chrome Open Chrome and click the three vertical dots in the top-right corner. Go to Settings > Privacy and security > Cookies and other site data . Choose your preferred option: Block all cookies (not recommended, can break most websites). Block third-party cookies (can block ads and tracking cookies). 2. Mozilla Firefox Open Firefox and click the three horizontal lines in the top-right corner. Go to Settings > Privacy & Security . Under the Enhanced Tracking Protection section, choose Strict to block most cookies or Custom to manually choose which cookies to block. 3. Safari Open Safari and click Safari in the top-left corner of the screen. Go to Preferences > Privacy . Check Block all cookies to stop all cookies, or select options to block third-party cookies. 4. Microsoft Edge Open Edge and click the three horizontal dots in the top-right corner. Go to Settings > Privacy, search, and services > Cookies and site permissions . Select your cookie settings from there, including blocking all cookies or blocking third-party cookies. 5. On Mobile (iOS/Android) For Safari on iOS: Go to Settings > Safari > Privacy & Security > Block All Cookies . For Chrome on Android: Open the app, tap the three dots, go to Settings > Privacy and security > Cookies . Be Aware: Disabling cookies can make your online experience more difficult. Some websites may not load properly, or you may be logged out frequently. Also, certain features may not work as expected. UConn University of Connecticut Search University of Connecticut Search UConn A to Z Index UConn A to Z Index Site A-Z UConn A-Z UConn Careers Search this Site Search in https://wp.newemployees.hr.uconn.edu/> Search UConn Careers Apply for Jobs Applicant Login Career Portal Help Subscribe to Job Alerts Refine Your Search Filter Results Work type Full-time 1 Locations UConn Storrs 1 Categories Student Programs and Services 1 Director of Engineering Student Engagement Apply now Search #: 499805 Work type: Full-time Location: UConn Storrs Categories: Student Programs and Services JOB SUMMARY The College of Engineering at the University of Connecticut seeks a full-time professional staff member to serve as Director of Engineering Student Engagement. As a student-facing position, the modality of this position is hybrid, with significant on-campus expectations and limited opportunities for remote work. Based on programming needs, attendance at occasional evening/weekend events may also be required. Reporting to the Associate Dean for Undergraduate Education, this position provides strategic leadership for high-impact educational experiences that enhance undergraduate engineering student engagement, success, leadership development, and global learning. The Director oversees several signature College initiatives, including the Engineering House Learning Community, the International Engineering Program, and engineering dual-degree partnerships. The position develops and manages programs that foster academic success, student belonging, intercultural competence, and leadership development, while collaborating with faculty, academic departments, Residential Life, Global Affairs, Student Affairs, and other campus partners. The Director supervises student staff and graduate assistants, manages program budgets, develops learning outcomes and assessment strategies, and supports continuous improvement efforts related to undergraduate student engagement initiatives and assigned program assessment activities. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Provides strategic leadership for signature student engagement programs and collaborates with College leadership to align goals, priorities, and assessment plans with the undergraduate engineering mission. Directs the International Engineering Program, including engineering and foreign language dual-degree partnerships and global learning opportunities for undergraduate students. Serves as Staff Director of the Engineering House Learning Community (EHLC), overseeing first-year and sophomore programming, community-building activities, and academic and social events that promote belonging and student success. In collaboration with the EHLC Faculty Director, develops and implements co-curricular programming that promotes student belonging, leadership development, intercultural competence, and academic success. Oversees staffing of UNIV courses associated with the EHLC, including teaching sections as needed. Collaborates with the Associate Dean, EHLC Assistant Director, faculty, academic departments (including partner language programs in CLAS), Residential Life, First-Year Programs and Learning Communities (FYPLC), Global Affairs, Student Affairs, and other campus partners to plan and implement student engagement initiatives. Supervises assigned personnel, including student staff, graduate assistants, and learning community leaders; establishes expectations, assigns work, evaluates performance, and supports professional growth. Manages annual budgets for assigned programs; approves expenditures, monitors spending, and prepares budget reports and projections. Leads assessment and evaluation of assigned programs, using data and feedback to improve program effectiveness, student experience, and learning outcomes; supports assessment activities as assigned. Serves as an authoritative resource to students, faculty, staff, and others on program policies, procedures, and eligibility; advises students on opportunities that support academic success and engagement. Recruits students for international engineering educational opportunities, tracks student interest, and in collaboration with the College of Engineering advising team, provides academic advising to students in study abroad programs. Develops and implements promotional and outreach strategies for engagement programs, including websites, informational materials, and student-facing events. Researches, drafts, and distributes policies and processes related to student engagement programs, ensuring clarity, consistency, and equity for all stakeholders. Prepares studies, reports, recommendations, and presentations for internal and external stakeholders regarding learning communities, international programs, and student success initiatives. At the direction of the Associate Dean, represents the College at meetings, events, trainings, and other outreach activities involving students, faculty, campus partners, and external collaborators. Serves on University committees and task forces related to undergraduate education, student success, learning communities, and global education. Collaborates with the Associate Dean on special projects or initiatives related to undergraduate education and student engagement. Performs related work as required. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Master's degree in higher education, student affairs, counseling, international education, engineering education, or a related field. Four years of related experience in higher education, student engagement, academic program administration, or student success programming. Demonstrated written communication, presentation, and interpersonal skills, with the ability to work effectively with students, faculty, staff, and campus partners. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS At least one year of supervisory or management experience. Demonstrated success leading and assessing student programs. Experience directing learning communities or living-learning programs. Experience administering international education or global engineering programs. Demonstrated knowledge of student development theories and evidence-based student success practices. Experience managing budgets and administrative operations. Demonstrated ability to develop, plan, and present programs and workshops. Demonstrated knowledge of assessment methods and continuous improvement in higher education. Demonstrated knowledge of online student records software, such as PeopleSoft. APPOINTMENT TERMS This is a full-time, 12-month, permanent position. The University offers a competitive salary, and outstanding benefits, including employee and dependent tuition waivers at UConn, and a highly desirable work environment.  For additional information regarding benefits, please visit UConn Health Benefits and explore the sections under the Benefits & Leaves and Engagement & Learning headers. Other rights, terms, and conditions of employment are contained in the collective bargaining agreement between the University of Connecticut and the University of Connecticut Professional Employees Association (UCPEA). TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF EMPLOYMENT Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. TO APPLY Please apply online at Faculty and Staff Positions , Search #499805 to upload as a PDF a resume , a cover letter , and contact information for three (3) professional references . Incomplete applications will not be considered. Applicants must clearly demonstrate how they meet the stated minimum qualifications, and any preferred qualifications they may possess, in their application materials. This job posting is scheduled to be removed at 11:55 p.m. Eastern time on September 25, 2026. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression . The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. Advertised: Aug 25 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Sep 25 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Ok, we will send you jobs like this. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Department Search # Location Closes Director of Engineering Student Engagement Engineering Undergrad Programs 499805 UConn Storrs Sep 25 2026 The College of Engineering at the University of Connecticut seeks a full-time professional staff member to serve as Director of Engineering Student Engagement. This position provides strategic leadership for high-impact educational experiences that enhance undergraduate engineering student engagement, success, leadership development, and global learning. Current opportunities Position Department Search # Location Closes Director of Engineering Student Engagement Engineering Undergrad Programs 499805 UConn Storrs Sep 25 2026 The College of Engineering at the University of Connecticut seeks a full-time professional staff member to serve as Director of Engineering Student Engagement. This position provides strategic leadership for high-impact educational experiences that enhance undergraduate engineering student engagement, success, leadership development, and global learning. Powered by PageUp © University of Connecticut Disclaimers, Privacy & Copyright Accessibility Webmaster Login"
