      "description": "UConn Careers | UConn Careers | University of Connecticut Our websites may use cookies to personalize and enhance your experience.  By continuing without changing your cookie settings, you agree to this collection.  For more information, please see our University Websites Privacy Notice . OK More Information Skip to Navigation Skip to UConn Search Skip to Content Our websites may use cookies to personalize and enhance your experience. By continuing without changing your cookie settings, you agree to this collection. For more information, please see our University Websites Privacy Notice . UConn Cookie Information What are Cookies? Authentication Cookies Analytics Cookies Opt Out Web cookies (also called HTTP cookies , browser cookies , or simply cookies ) are small pieces of data that websites store on your device (computer, phone, etc.) through your web browser. They are used to remember information about you and your interactions with the site. Purpose of Cookies: Session Management : Keeping you logged in Remembering items in a shopping cart Saving language or theme preferences Personalization : Tailoring content or ads based on your previous activity Tracking & Analytics : Monitoring browsing behavior for analytics or marketing purposes Types of Cookies: Session Cookies : Temporary; deleted when you close your browser Used for things like keeping you logged in during a single session Persistent Cookies : Stored on your device until they expire or are manually deleted Used for remembering login credentials, settings, etc. First-Party Cookies : Set by the website you're visiting directly Third-Party Cookies : Set by other domains (usually advertisers) embedded in the website Commonly used for tracking across multiple sites Authentication cookies are a special type of web cookie used to identify and verify a user after they log in to a website or web application. What They Do: Once you log in to a site, the server creates an authentication cookie and sends it to your browser. This cookie: Proves to the website that you're logged in Prevents you from having to log in again on every page you visit Can persist across sessions if you select \"Remember me\" What's Inside an Authentication Cookie? Typically, it contains: A unique session ID (not your actual password) Optional metadata (e.g., expiration time, security flags) Analytics cookies are cookies used to collect data about how visitors interact with a website . Their primary purpose is to help website owners understand and improve user experience by analyzing things like: How users navigate the site Which pages are most/least visited How long users stay on each page What device, browser, or location the user is from What They Track: Some examples of data analytics cookies may collect: Page views and time spent on pages Click paths (how users move from page to page) Bounce rate (users who leave without interacting) User demographics (location, language, device) Referring websites (how users arrived at the site) Here’s how you can disable cookies in common browsers: 1. Google Chrome Open Chrome and click the three vertical dots in the top-right corner. Go to Settings > Privacy and security > Cookies and other site data . Choose your preferred option: Block all cookies (not recommended, can break most websites). Block third-party cookies (can block ads and tracking cookies). 2. Mozilla Firefox Open Firefox and click the three horizontal lines in the top-right corner. Go to Settings > Privacy & Security . Under the Enhanced Tracking Protection section, choose Strict to block most cookies or Custom to manually choose which cookies to block. 3. Safari Open Safari and click Safari in the top-left corner of the screen. Go to Preferences > Privacy . Check Block all cookies to stop all cookies, or select options to block third-party cookies. 4. Microsoft Edge Open Edge and click the three horizontal dots in the top-right corner. Go to Settings > Privacy, search, and services > Cookies and site permissions . Select your cookie settings from there, including blocking all cookies or blocking third-party cookies. 5. On Mobile (iOS/Android) For Safari on iOS: Go to Settings > Safari > Privacy & Security > Block All Cookies . For Chrome on Android: Open the app, tap the three dots, go to Settings > Privacy and security > Cookies . Be Aware: Disabling cookies can make your online experience more difficult. Some websites may not load properly, or you may be logged out frequently. Also, certain features may not work as expected. UConn University of Connecticut Search University of Connecticut Search UConn A to Z Index UConn A to Z Index Site A-Z UConn A-Z UConn Careers Search this Site Search in https://wp.newemployees.hr.uconn.edu/> Search UConn Careers Apply for Jobs Applicant Login Career Portal Help Subscribe to Job Alerts Refine Your Search Filter Results Work type Full-time 1 Locations UConn Storrs 1 Categories Faculty Department Head/Director 1 Professor and Department Head Apply now Search #: 499673 Work type: Full-time Location: UConn Storrs Categories: Faculty Department Head/Director JOB SUMMARY The University of Connecticut seeks a dynamic leader to serve as Head of the Department of Social and Critical Inquiry (SCI). This department is a new interdisciplinary unit that was established in 2024 through collaborative efforts of faculty across its four areas: American Studies; Asian and Asian American Studies; Native American and Indigenous Studies; and Women’s, Gender, and Sexuality Studies, which includes a focus on Feminist Disability Studies.  This position is the second among five additional lines planned for the Department. The next Department Head will provide strategic leadership to guide the development and implementation of a shared and coherent academic program. The department is dedicated to public engagement and interdisciplinary research, and we therefore solicit applications from academic leaders who possess wide-ranging administrative skills, expertise in team building, experience advancing collaborative research, and directing academic innovation. The successful candidate will be appointed fully in Social and Critical Inquiry at the rank of Professor and will be expected to lead the department’s areas by strengthening the academic and professional success of its faculty, students, and staff, and developing its diverse, vibrant, and inclusive intellectual community. For this position, we seek applicants who teach and research in the Department’s areas or in related fields. Successful candidates will have a strong record of publication demonstrating interdisciplinary excellence and a track record of collaborative faculty initiatives that could be public, tribal, and/or community-partnered in nature. The University of Connecticut is a public land-grant institution that acknowledges that the land on which we gather is the territory of the Eastern Pequot, Golden Hill Paugussett, Lenape, Mashantucket Pequot, Mohegan, Nipmuc and Schaghticoke Peoples who have stewarded this land throughout the generations. We thank them for their strength and resilience in protecting this land and aspire to uphold our responsibilities according to their example. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES The Department Head works collaboratively with the General Council of Area Directors. The responsibilities of the SCI Department Head include work across the following dimensions: Strategic Leadership and Department Development Academic Program Leadership and Planning Governance and Department Administration Budget and Staffing Management Faculty Development and Mentoring Student Success and Educational Impact Community Partnerships and Public Facing Scholarship MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Ph.D. (or equivalent) in a primary field in one or more of the Area’s within the department or a related field. Demonstrated leadership experience at the Department, Unit, or Dean’s level, including program building and curricular innovation. Demonstrated teaching and interdisciplinary research excellence. Demonstrated experience with supporting engaged scholarship and/or community partnerships. Demonstrated commitment to advancing diversity via research, teaching, scholarship, or in other ways. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Demonstrated experience supporting community partnerships with Native American and Indigenous communities. Demonstrated teaching and research experience in WGSS fields. Budget experience with preference for RCM budgeting models. Interest or experience in working in federally designated Minority Serving Institutions. Experience in grant application and management of grant-funded activities. APPOINTMENT TERMS This is a full-time, 10-month, tenure-track position with an anticipated start date of August 23, 2027. The successful candidate’s academic appointment will be at the Storrs campus. Faculty may also be asked to teach at one of UConn’s regional campuses as part of their ordinary workload. Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Applicants must meet University requirements for appointment at the rank of full Professor, with tenure. TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF EMPLOYMENT Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. TO APPLY Please apply online at Faculty and Staff Positions , Search #499673 and submit the following application materials: A cover letter , Curriculum vitae , Research and scholarship statement (innovative concepts that will form the basis of academic career, experience in proposal development, mentorship of graduate students, etc.); Teaching statement (including teaching philosophy, teaching experience, commitment to effective learning, concepts for new course development, etc.); Commitment to diversity statement (including broadening participation, integrating multicultural experiences in instruction and research and pedagogical techniques to meet the needs of diverse learning styles, etc.); Sample journal articles or books Name and Contact information for 3 professional reference s. For more information regarding the Department of Social and Critical Inquiry please visit our department website . Questions may be directed to clas@uconn.edu . At the University of Connecticut, our commitment to excellence is complemented by our commitment to building a culturally diverse community. This job posting is scheduled to be removed at 11:55 p.m. Eastern time on October 16, 2026. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression . The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. Advertised: Aug 11 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Oct 16 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Ok, we will send you jobs like this. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Department Search # Location Closes Professor and Department Head Social and Critical Inquiry 499673 UConn Storrs Oct 16 2026 The University of Connecticut seeks a dynamic leader to serve as Head of the Department of Social and Critical Inquiry (SCI). Current opportunities Position Department Search # Location Closes Professor and Department Head Social and Critical Inquiry 499673 UConn Storrs Oct 16 2026 The University of Connecticut seeks a dynamic leader to serve as Head of the Department of Social and Critical Inquiry (SCI). Powered by PageUp © University of Connecticut Disclaimers, Privacy & Copyright Accessibility Webmaster Login"
    },
    "https://jobs.hr.uconn.edu/cw/en-us/job/499768/web-application-developer": {
      "title": "UConn Cookie Information",
      "description": "UConn Careers | UConn Careers | University of Connecticut Our websites may use cookies to personalize and enhance your experience.  By continuing without changing your cookie settings, you agree to this collection.  For more information, please see our University Websites Privacy Notice . OK More Information Skip to Navigation Skip to UConn Search Skip to Content Our websites may use cookies to personalize and enhance your experience. By continuing without changing your cookie settings, you agree to this collection. For more information, please see our University Websites Privacy Notice . UConn Cookie Information What are Cookies? Authentication Cookies Analytics Cookies Opt Out Web cookies (also called HTTP cookies , browser cookies , or simply cookies ) are small pieces of data that websites store on your device (computer, phone, etc.) through your web browser. They are used to remember information about you and your interactions with the site. Purpose of Cookies: Session Management : Keeping you logged in Remembering items in a shopping cart Saving language or theme preferences Personalization : Tailoring content or ads based on your previous activity Tracking & Analytics : Monitoring browsing behavior for analytics or marketing purposes Types of Cookies: Session Cookies : Temporary; deleted when you close your browser Used for things like keeping you logged in during a single session Persistent Cookies : Stored on your device until they expire or are manually deleted Used for remembering login credentials, settings, etc. First-Party Cookies : Set by the website you're visiting directly Third-Party Cookies : Set by other domains (usually advertisers) embedded in the website Commonly used for tracking across multiple sites Authentication cookies are a special type of web cookie used to identify and verify a user after they log in to a website or web application. What They Do: Once you log in to a site, the server creates an authentication cookie and sends it to your browser. This cookie: Proves to the website that you're logged in Prevents you from having to log in again on every page you visit Can persist across sessions if you select \"Remember me\" What's Inside an Authentication Cookie? Typically, it contains: A unique session ID (not your actual password) Optional metadata (e.g., expiration time, security flags) Analytics cookies are cookies used to collect data about how visitors interact with a website . Their primary purpose is to help website owners understand and improve user experience by analyzing things like: How users navigate the site Which pages are most/least visited How long users stay on each page What device, browser, or location the user is from What They Track: Some examples of data analytics cookies may collect: Page views and time spent on pages Click paths (how users move from page to page) Bounce rate (users who leave without interacting) User demographics (location, language, device) Referring websites (how users arrived at the site) Here’s how you can disable cookies in common browsers: 1. Google Chrome Open Chrome and click the three vertical dots in the top-right corner. Go to Settings > Privacy and security > Cookies and other site data . Choose your preferred option: Block all cookies (not recommended, can break most websites). Block third-party cookies (can block ads and tracking cookies). 2. Mozilla Firefox Open Firefox and click the three horizontal lines in the top-right corner. Go to Settings > Privacy & Security . Under the Enhanced Tracking Protection section, choose Strict to block most cookies or Custom to manually choose which cookies to block. 3. Safari Open Safari and click Safari in the top-left corner of the screen. Go to Preferences > Privacy . Check Block all cookies to stop all cookies, or select options to block third-party cookies. 4. Microsoft Edge Open Edge and click the three horizontal dots in the top-right corner. Go to Settings > Privacy, search, and services > Cookies and site permissions . Select your cookie settings from there, including blocking all cookies or blocking third-party cookies. 5. On Mobile (iOS/Android) For Safari on iOS: Go to Settings > Safari > Privacy & Security > Block All Cookies . For Chrome on Android: Open the app, tap the three dots, go to Settings > Privacy and security > Cookies . Be Aware: Disabling cookies can make your online experience more difficult. Some websites may not load properly, or you may be logged out frequently. Also, certain features may not work as expected. UConn University of Connecticut Search University of Connecticut Search UConn A to Z Index UConn A to Z Index Site A-Z UConn A-Z UConn Careers Search this Site Search in https://wp.newemployees.hr.uconn.edu/> Search UConn Careers Apply for Jobs Applicant Login Career Portal Help Subscribe to Job Alerts Refine Your Search Filter Results Work type Full-time 1 Locations UConn Storrs 1 Categories Information Technology 1 Web Application Developer Apply now Search #: 499768 Work type: Full-time Location: UConn Storrs Categories: Information Technology JOB SUMMARY Institutional Insights & Innovation (i3) is seeking an experienced Web Application Developer (Software Developer 2) to help design, build, deploy, and support a growing portfolio of institutional applications and digital platforms. This position is ideal for a versatile, experienced developer who enjoys working across the full lifecycle of a software product: understanding a problem, designing a solution, writing application code, integrating systems and data, deploying applications, troubleshooting production environments, and continually improving the systems they support. The Web Application Developer will work closely with developers, designers, product and project staff, student contributors, and partners across the University. The successful candidate will contribute directly to major institutional initiatives while also helping strengthen the technical practices and infrastructure that allow i3 to develop and operate software reliably at scale. The role requires strong application development skills as well as the ability to work comfortably across the broader technical environment surrounding modern web applications. This may include Linux application environments, web servers, databases, authentication systems, APIs, deployment workflows, background processes, logging, monitoring, and other components necessary to move software successfully from development into production. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Design, develop, test, deploy, maintain, and enhance production of web applications and institutional software platforms. Develop server-side applications using modern web application frameworks, with an emphasis on maintainable, secure, and well-structured software. Work collaboratively with designers, developers, product and project staff, and university stakeholders to translate institutional needs into effective technical solutions. Develop and maintain integrations with institutional data sources, APIs, authentication systems, third-party services, and other enterprise technologies. Design and work with relational databases, data models, queries, and application-level data integrations. Support the deployment and operation of web applications in Linux-based server environments. Troubleshoot complex technical issues across application code, web servers, databases, operating systems, integrations, authentication services, and related infrastructure. Contribute to the development and improvement of deployment processes, automation, CI/CD workflows, logging, monitoring, and other DevOps practices. Help ensure applications meet appropriate standards for security, performance, reliability, usability, and accessibility. Participate in architectural and technical design decisions for new and existing applications. Conduct code reviews and contribute to shared software development standards and technical best practices. Provide technical guidance and mentorship to junior developers and student developers. Create and maintain appropriate technical documentation to support application development, deployment, maintenance, and operational continuity. Collaborate with University technology partners on infrastructure, networking, identity and access management, security, hosting, and related technical needs. Evaluate emerging technologies and development approaches that may improve i3’s ability to deliver effective institutional solutions. Perform other related duties in support of i3’s evolving portfolio of digital initiatives. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree in Computer Science, Information Technology, Software Engineering, or a related field and at least four years of professional software development experience, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Experience developing server-side applications using a modern web application framework. Experience working with relational databases, SQL, and data-driven applications. Experience using Git or another distributed version control system in a collaborative software development environment. Experience troubleshooting technical issues across multiple components of a web application environment, including application code, web servers, databases, integrations, or operating systems. Experience developing or integrating APIs, authentication systems, or other external services. Experience using AI tooling (e.g., Copilot, Claude, Cursor, ChatGPT) to accelerate development. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Professional experience with PHP and the Laravel framework. Experience administering or supporting Linux-based application environments, including technologies such as Apache or Nginx, PHP, system services, scheduled processes, background queues, file permissions, and application logging. Experience with automated deployment, CI/CD pipelines, configuration management, containerization, or other DevOps practices. Experience troubleshooting and configuring application infrastructure involving DNS, TLS/SSL certificates, networking, firewalls, web servers, or reverse proxies. Experience implementing or supporting enterprise authentication and identity technologies such as SAML, OpenID Connect, OAuth, LDAP, or single sign-on. Experience with application monitoring, logging, backup and recovery processes, and production incident troubleshooting. APPOINTMENT TERMS This is a full-time, end-dated position with the possibility of renewal subject to performance and funding. The University offers a competitive salary, and outstanding benefits , including employee and dependent tuition waivers at UConn, and a highly desirable work environment. Other rights, terms, and conditions of employment are contained in the collective bargaining agreement between the University of Connecticut and the University of Connecticut Professional Employees Association (UCPEA). TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF EMPLOYMENT Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. TO APPLY Please apply online at Faculty and Staff Positions , Search #499768 to upload a resume , cover letter , and contact information for three (3) professional references . Applicants must clearly demonstrate how they meet the stated minimum qualifications, and any preferred qualifications they may possess, in their application materials. This job posting is scheduled to be removed at 11:55 p.m. Eastern time on September 11, 2026. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression . The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. Advertised: Aug 11 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Sep 11 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Ok, we will send you jobs like this. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Department Search # Location Closes Web Application Developer Institutional Innovation 499768 UConn Storrs Sep 11 2026 Institutional Insights & Innovation (i3) is a small, agile digital enablement group embedded within the academic and research context at UConn. i3 is seeking an experienced Web Application Developer (Software Developer 2) to help design, build, deploy, and support a growing portfolio of institutional applications and digital platforms. This position is ideal for a versatile, experienced developer who enjoys working across the full lifecycle of a software product: understanding a problem, designing a solution, writing application code, integrating systems and data, deploying applications, troubleshooting production environments, and continually improving the systems they support. Current opportunities Position Department Search # Location Closes Web Application Developer Institutional Innovation 499768 UConn Storrs Sep 11 2026 Institutional Insights & Innovation (i3) is a small, agile digital enablement group embedded within the academic and research context at UConn. i3 is seeking an experienced Web Application Developer (Software Developer 2) to help design, build, deploy, and support a growing portfolio of institutional applications and digital platforms. This position is ideal for a versatile, experienced developer who enjoys working across the full lifecycle of a software product: understanding a problem, designing a solution, writing application code, integrating systems and data, deploying applications, troubleshooting production environments, and continually improving the systems they support. Powered by PageUp © University of Connecticut Disclaimers, Privacy & Copyright Accessibility Webmaster Login"
